00:00:01.390 --> 00:00:03.940
This is a risky segment for me to do.

00:00:03.940 --> 00:00:05.560
Why is it risky?

00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:10.670
It's risky because it's wonky and it's technical,
and I don't know that people will like it.

00:00:10.670 --> 00:00:14.930
This might be the type of segment that ends
up with, like, you know, 5000 views on YouTube,

00:00:14.930 --> 00:00:19.019
but about 10 people have emailed me and they've
asked me to do this.

00:00:19.019 --> 00:00:20.019
They've requested this.

00:00:20.019 --> 00:00:21.019
So let's do it.

00:00:21.019 --> 00:00:26.550
We are going to talk about two mechanisms
which Donald Trump and other rich people can

00:00:26.550 --> 00:00:29.960
use to reduce the amount they pay in taxes.

00:00:29.960 --> 00:00:33.990
The two mechanisms are tax loss, carry forwards.

00:00:33.990 --> 00:00:35.780
Please don't glaze over.

00:00:35.780 --> 00:00:39.620
Tax loss, carry forwards and asset depreciation.

00:00:39.620 --> 00:00:45.590
OK, so Donald Trump's taxes were leaked by
this blockbuster New York Times report in

00:00:45.590 --> 00:00:47.160
that New York Times report.

00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:54.390
We learned that there were some years like
2016 and 2017 when Donald Trump paid 750 dollars

00:00:54.390 --> 00:01:01.140
in total federal taxes total for the entire
year in 10 of the last 15 years.

00:01:01.140 --> 00:01:05.100
Donald Trump paid zero in federal taxes.

00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:07.460
How on earth is this possible?

00:01:07.460 --> 00:01:15.119
Well, it's possible, thanks to something called
a tax loss carry forward and asset depreciation.

00:01:15.119 --> 00:01:17.510
I'm going to explain both.

00:01:17.510 --> 00:01:22.520
And you are very quickly going to see how
the tax code favors the super rich, as if

00:01:22.520 --> 00:01:24.290
you didn't already know that.

00:01:24.290 --> 00:01:30.060
So a tax loss carry forward allows you to
take a loss that happened in one year and

00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:35.200
carry it forward to future years to reduce
the amount of tax that you owe.

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:40.680
So let's do like a very pedestrian example
compared to what Donald Trump is doing.

00:01:40.680 --> 00:01:42.580
We'll do it with stocks.

00:01:42.580 --> 00:01:48.560
Imagine that you buy twenty thousand dollars
of some stock in one year and it loses 50

00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:49.590
percent of its value.

00:01:49.590 --> 00:01:52.700
So you're 20000 dollars is now only 10000
dollars.

00:01:52.700 --> 00:01:54.880
You lost 10000 dollars.

00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:55.880
Great.

00:01:55.880 --> 00:01:56.880
You can deduct losses.

00:01:56.880 --> 00:02:00.040
You can deduct ten thousand dollars from your
taxes.

00:02:00.040 --> 00:02:03.890
From the standpoint of paying capital gains
and it counts against your gains.

00:02:03.890 --> 00:02:04.890
Beautiful.

00:02:04.890 --> 00:02:10.759
But imagine that in the year that you lost
the 10 grand and sold the stock and realized

00:02:10.759 --> 00:02:16.530
that loss, you only made two thousand dollars
from all of your other stock sales.

00:02:16.530 --> 00:02:17.670
So you've got this.

00:02:17.670 --> 00:02:19.459
Ten thousand dollar loss.

00:02:19.459 --> 00:02:21.150
And only a two thousand dollar gain.

00:02:21.150 --> 00:02:22.650
Well, great news.

00:02:22.650 --> 00:02:27.550
You don't have to use the full ten thousand
dollar loss in that year.

00:02:27.550 --> 00:02:29.680
You had a two thousand dollar gain.

00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:34.080
You'll take just two thousand dollars from
the 10 that you lost.

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:38.030
And you offset a two thousand dollar gain
with a two thousand dollar loss.

00:02:38.030 --> 00:02:39.030
You're at zero.

00:02:39.030 --> 00:02:45.650
You pay no capital gains and your other eight
thousand dollars in losses can be carried

00:02:45.650 --> 00:02:48.050
over to future years.

00:02:48.050 --> 00:02:54.190
Now, maybe the next year you have a really
good year in stocks and you realize seven

00:02:54.190 --> 00:02:56.050
thousand dollars in gains.

00:02:56.050 --> 00:03:01.069
Well, you still have the eight thousand dollars
in loss that you carried over.

00:03:01.069 --> 00:03:05.630
You can use another 7000 to offset the money
you made in stocks.

00:03:05.630 --> 00:03:08.549
And again, you pay zero.

00:03:08.549 --> 00:03:13.660
Now, that sounds reasonable enough and it's
simple enough, but that is small potatoes.

00:03:13.660 --> 00:03:16.319
That's not how Trump takes advantage of it.

00:03:16.319 --> 00:03:21.290
Real losses on the sale of stock are not what
Donald Trump is doing.

00:03:21.290 --> 00:03:28.610
Donald Trump is generating paper losses with
capital losses from huge hotels and casinos.

00:03:28.610 --> 00:03:30.409
So here is an example.

00:03:30.409 --> 00:03:38.270
In 1995, Donald Trump reported a loss of 916
million dollars on a casino.

00:03:38.270 --> 00:03:41.269
And we'll talk about how that loss was generated.

00:03:41.269 --> 00:03:49.160
He then gets to take that 960 million dollar
loss and offset 50 million dollars in taxes

00:03:49.160 --> 00:03:55.060
per year for 18 years in 2008 and 2009.

00:03:55.060 --> 00:04:02.019
Donald Trump declared a loss of one point
four billion dollars, and he extended the

00:04:02.019 --> 00:04:09.130
ability to carry these losses forward indefinitely
in his own tax plan, which was made effective

00:04:09.130 --> 00:04:10.629
in 2018.

00:04:10.629 --> 00:04:17.290
So Trump can take that one point four billion
dollar loss and just carry it forward indefinitely,

00:04:17.290 --> 00:04:18.830
offsetting future taxes.

00:04:18.830 --> 00:04:24.020
And the trick of it is that you can also mess
with how you declare these losses.

00:04:24.020 --> 00:04:30.610
So we imagine listen, I buy something for
a thousand bucks and I sell it for five hundred.

00:04:30.610 --> 00:04:32.880
I really lost 500 dollars.

00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:38.530
That's a real loss of 500 dollars with these
huge real estate transactions and capital

00:04:38.530 --> 00:04:39.530
investments.

00:04:39.530 --> 00:04:43.860
There are all sorts of ways that you can move
around assets and mess with the valuation

00:04:43.860 --> 00:04:44.860
of the assets.

00:04:44.860 --> 00:04:50.350
I'll get back to that later to generate losses
on paper, which didn't really happen in the

00:04:50.350 --> 00:04:51.350
way you were.

00:04:51.350 --> 00:04:57.020
I think about losing money and you're generating
these paper losses, which you can then carry

00:04:57.020 --> 00:04:58.020
forward.

00:04:58.020 --> 00:04:59.980
Your average middle class working person can't
do this.

00:04:59.980 --> 00:05:02.550
Now, the other tactic Donald Trump uses.

00:05:02.550 --> 00:05:03.550
Where do you.

00:05:03.550 --> 00:05:09.890
Generate these losses to carry forward, one
of the ways you can do it is with asset depreciation.

00:05:09.890 --> 00:05:15.400
Depreciation means the value of an asset has
gone down five a car that's worth, you know,

00:05:15.400 --> 00:05:17.980
fifteen thousand dollars when I buy it.

00:05:17.980 --> 00:05:20.850
And after a couple years, it's worth ten thousand
dollars.

00:05:20.850 --> 00:05:23.710
That's depreciated by 5000 dollars.

00:05:23.710 --> 00:05:30.320
Now, with a huge real estate portfolio, you
know, a hotel, a casino, a big office building,

00:05:30.320 --> 00:05:34.070
you don't just buy it and deduct it as a business
expense.

00:05:34.070 --> 00:05:37.470
Like I would do with, like an audio cable
or, you know, I bought this new microphone

00:05:37.470 --> 00:05:38.470
this year.

00:05:38.470 --> 00:05:41.550
I'm not going to depreciate this microphone
over the next however many years.

00:05:41.550 --> 00:05:43.040
It's too small of an expense.

00:05:43.040 --> 00:05:46.290
I'm just going to take it as a business expense
and deduct it right away.

00:05:46.290 --> 00:05:52.290
But with a huge hotel, you claim to have a
hotel worth five hundred million dollars.

00:05:52.290 --> 00:05:55.800
And then now you claim, you know, the hotel
has aged, the plumbing is older.

00:05:55.800 --> 00:05:58.330
You know, you've got to flush the toilets
10 to 15 times.

00:05:58.330 --> 00:05:59.330
As Trump says.

00:05:59.330 --> 00:06:02.270
You could say the hotel was worth five hundred
million.

00:06:02.270 --> 00:06:04.490
It's now worth 250 million.

00:06:04.490 --> 00:06:09.780
I can now take 250 million dollars in depreciation
and reduce my taxes.

00:06:09.780 --> 00:06:12.500
And this is where the manipulations come into
play.

00:06:12.500 --> 00:06:17.050
Michael Cohen has explained to us how Trump
does this without actually changing anything

00:06:17.050 --> 00:06:18.520
in the real world.

00:06:18.520 --> 00:06:25.220
If I can claim, you know, my 500 million dollar
hotel, it's really worth 600 million.

00:06:25.220 --> 00:06:32.630
So what that means is when it goes down in
value to 250, I'm not depreciating 250 million.

00:06:32.630 --> 00:06:40.300
I can now depreciate 350 million dollars and
create a loss carry forward that I can use

00:06:40.300 --> 00:06:42.050
for the next however many years.

00:06:42.050 --> 00:06:43.400
It's all imaginary.

00:06:43.400 --> 00:06:46.020
It's all merely pushing paper.

00:06:46.020 --> 00:06:47.630
It's accounting mechanisms.

00:06:47.630 --> 00:06:49.770
And we know Trump did this.

00:06:49.770 --> 00:06:51.970
The taxes prove it.

00:06:51.970 --> 00:06:57.410
And Michael Cohen and others have told us
Donald Trump for publicity purposes or for

00:06:57.410 --> 00:07:01.340
tax purposes or for whatever reason to get
a loan.

00:07:01.340 --> 00:07:06.710
Trump will inflate asset values when it's
convenient or deflate asset values when it's

00:07:06.710 --> 00:07:07.770
convenient.

00:07:07.770 --> 00:07:10.030
These are the mechanisms Trump has used.

00:07:10.030 --> 00:07:11.030
This is how they work.

00:07:11.030 --> 00:07:11.770
Hopefully, it's useful to folks to understand
that

